course: biology
lesson: 222
title: Rats and mice in the city, and the elephant and mouse myth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Wherever people live, brown rats and house mice live too, in drains, sheds, walls and under floors, eating the food we drop and throw away.
explain: A rat can squeeze through a hole about the size of a coin, swim through a pipe and hold its breath for minutes, and its front teeth never stop growing, so it gnaws all the time, even through cables.
explain: Rats are clever and careful: offered a new food, a rat nibbles a tiny bit and waits to see whether it feels ill, and in experiments rats have freed a trapped friend even when there was chocolate on offer.
explain: In Africa, big pouched rats are trained to sniff out buried landmines, saving people's lives, because they are light enough not to set the mines off.
explain: And the cartoon elephant that screams and jumps on a chair at the sight of a mouse? There is no good evidence that real elephants are especially afraid of mice.
fact: Brown rats live wherever people live; they eat our waste and shelter in drains, sewers and buildings.
fact: A rat can squeeze through a hole about the size of a coin, because its ribs are flexible and its skull decides what fits.
fact: Rats are good swimmers and can hold their breath for a few minutes, so they can swim through pipes.
fact: Fleas carried by rats helped spread the plague called the Black Death, but scientists now think human fleas and lice also spread it from person to person.
fact: Rats are cautious with new food: they nibble a little and wait to see whether they feel ill.
fact: In experiments, rats opened a cage to free another trapped rat, even when they could have eaten chocolate instead.
fact: Real rats have a superb sense of smell and taste, and they learn which foods are safe by smelling the breath of other rats.
fact: A rat's front teeth never stop growing, so it gnaws to keep them short; the teeth are tough enough to chew through wood, plastic and cables.
fact: House mice come indoors in winter for warmth and food, and eat crumbs, seeds, grain and sweet foods, which they like better than cheese.
fact: There is no good evidence that real elephants are especially scared of mice; like many animals, they may startle at any small sudden movement near their feet.
fact: African giant pouched rats are trained to sniff out landmines; they are light enough not to set them off and their noses are excellent.
fact: A rat or a mouse has four legs, a long tail and two pairs of long front teeth, one pair on top and one below.
exercise: Why do brown rats live in cities? | They eat our waste and shelter in drains and buildings | They are kept there by the city council | They cannot survive anywhere else in the whole world | They are lost and cannot find their way back to the countryside
exercise: How big a hole can a rat squeeze through? | About the size of a coin | Only as big as a door | About the size of a football | Only as big as its whole body when stretched
exercise: Why can a rat fit through such a small gap? | Its ribs are flexible and only its skull must fit | Its bones turn soft and bendy when it is scared | It can take its legs off | It squeezes all the air out of its fur
exercise: How can a rat get through a flooded pipe? | It swims well and holds its breath for minutes | It drinks all the water in the pipe first | It breathes water like a fish | It waits for the pipe to dry out, however long it takes
exercise: What spread the Black Death plague? | Fleas from rats, and probably human fleas and lice too | Rats biting people in their sleep | Eating cheese that mice had touched | Cats that ate the sick rats and then breathed on people
exercise: What does a rat do when it finds a new kind of food? | Nibbles a little and waits to see if it feels ill | Eats it all at once before other rats come | Never touches any new food ever | Buries it and forgets where it is
exercise: What did rats do in experiments with a trapped rat? | Opened the cage to free it, even with chocolate on offer | Ate the chocolate and ignored the trapped rat | Fought the trapped rat for the chocolate | Ran away as fast as they could
exercise: How do rats learn which foods are safe to eat? | By smelling the breath of other rats | By reading the labels on food packets | By watching which foods the cats eat first | By listening to what people say in the kitchen
exercise: Why does a rat gnaw through things like cables? | Its front teeth never stop growing, so it keeps them short | It wants to switch the lights off | It likes the taste of electricity | It is trying to build a nest of wires to keep its babies warm
exercise: Why do house mice come into the kitchen in winter? | For warmth and food | To lay eggs in the oven | Because they hate snow on their tails | To hide from the rats outside
exercise: Are real elephants terrified of mice? | No good evidence; they may startle at any small sudden movement | Yes, every elephant runs from a mouse | Yes, because mice climb up elephants' trunks and tickle them | No, elephants eat mice
exercise: Why are African giant pouched rats used to find landmines? | They have great noses and are too light to set mines off | They can dig mines up with their teeth | They can see through the ground with special eyes | They are not scared of loud bangs, so they do not mind if a mine goes off
exercise: How many legs does a rat have? | Four | Six, like a beetle | Only two | Eight
exercise: Which sense is a rat's best? | Smell | Sight in the dark | Hearing only | It has no good senses
test: «there's 3 rats in the alley, how many tails n legs» | 3 tails and 12 legs | 3 tails and 6 legs | 6 tails and 12 legs | 3 tails and 9 legs
test: In a famous cartoon a frightened elephant leaps onto a stool when a mouse runs past. What is true? | There is no good evidence real elephants fear mice especially | Real elephants always faint when they see a mouse | Real elephants fear mice because mice eat their food | Real mice hunt elephants in the wild, so elephants have learned to fear them
test: In a film a rat called Remy becomes a great cook with his amazing nose. What part is close to real life? | Real rats have a superb sense of smell and taste | Real rats can cook food in an oven | Real rats can steer a person by pulling hair | Real rats can read recipes
test: «blocked the hole with my thumb-sized bit of foam, rat can't get in now, sorted» What is likely? | A rat can still gnaw through it or find a coin-sized gap | Rats never get through anything soft | Foam is poisonous so rats run away | Rats cannot climb, so a hole off the ground is always safe
test: «put new poison bait down, rats ate a tiny bit then left it, they're thick or what» What is really going on? | Rats are careful and test a little of new food first | Rats only eat at full moon | Rats eat only cheese, never anything else | Rats did not notice the bait at all, because they cannot smell
test: «my grandad says rats alone gave everyone the Black Death» Is he completely right? | Not quite; rat fleas helped, but human fleas and lice probably spread it too | Yes, rats bit every person who caught it | No, cats spread it, not rats | No, the Black Death was caused by bad weather and dirty air, not by any animal
test: What would happen to a pet rat that was given nothing hard to chew for months? | Its front teeth could grow too long | Its teeth would fall out and grow back | Nothing, rats' teeth stop growing | Its tail would grow longer instead
test: Count them: a mouse family has 2 parents and 6 babies. How many front teeth do they have, with 4 each? | 32 | 8 | 24 | 16
test: «why's the mouse only come in when it's freezing out??» | It wants warmth and food for the winter | Mice only like the dark of winter | Snow makes mice angry | It is coming in to hibernate in the fridge
test: In Tom and Jerry a mouse runs off with a big wedge of holey cheese. What do real mice like most? | Seeds, grain and sweet foods | Only yellow cheese with holes in it | Only raw meat and fish from bins | Nothing; they never eat indoors
test: What would most likely happen if a giant pouched rat walked over a buried landmine? | Nothing, it is too light to set it off | It would set it off and be blown up at once | It would try to gnaw through the landmine | It would dig the mine up and carry it away
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the rat is capable of passing through an aperture of very small diameter. | The ribs are flexible, so that only the skull determines the passage | The skeleton is composed entirely of cartilage | The animal is able to detach its limbs temporarily | The fur may be compressed to a fraction of its thickness
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the popular belief that elephants fear mice? | No good evidence supports a particular fear of mice in elephants | It has been demonstrated in all elephants under all conditions | It arises because mice habitually enter the trunk of the elephant | It arises because mice compete with elephants for food
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the behaviour of the rat when presented with an unfamiliar food. | A small quantity is consumed and the animal awaits any ill effect | The entire quantity is consumed without delay, before competitors arrive | The food is invariably rejected without examination | The food is concealed and subsequently forgotten
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the African giant pouched rat is employed in the detection of landmines. | Its acute sense of smell is combined with a body weight insufficient to detonate them | Its eyesight permits the observation of objects beneath the soil | Its teeth permit the safe dismantling of the devices once they have been located | Its hearing permits the detection of ticking mechanisms
